---
/**
 * Public Ed Sites case study overlay (Figma node 246:1578).
 *
 * The long-form variant: prose sections with supporting assets, the two
 * Figma prototypes from the deck played inline (the landing page after the
 * public-site sections, the admin after the admin sections), and a closing
 * banner out to the full deck. Opened by the `teal` carousel card.
 *
 * Header, copy, and hero come from the registry entry so this study reads the
 * same on the card and in the overlay; only the body blocks live here.
 */
import CaseStudyOverlay from "../CaseStudyOverlay.astro";
import ProseBlock from "../case-study/ProseBlock.astro";
import AssetRow from "../case-study/AssetRow.astro";
import DeckEmbed from "../case-study/DeckEmbed.astro";
import Banner from "../case-study/Banner.astro";
import { publicEdSites, toOverlayProps } from "../../lib/case-studies";

import deckCover from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/deck-cover.png";
import boardableProduct from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/boardable-product.png";
import replitPrototypeExample from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/replit-prototype-example.png";
import existingSites from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/existing-sites.png";
import componentLibrary from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/component-library.png";
import homePage from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/home-page.png";
import emptyNoCustomizations from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/empty-state-no-customizations.png";
import emptyNoFeaturedMeetings from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/empty-state-no-featured-meetings.png";
import emptyNoPolicies from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/empty-state-no-policies.png";
import theming from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/theming.png";
import meetingsList from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/meetings-list.png";
import sideBySideAgenda from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/side-by-side-agenda.png";
import policyBook from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/policy-book.png";
import siteSearch from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/site-search.png";
import prototypeLandingCover from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/prototype-landing-cover.png";
import adminPolicies from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/admin-policies.png";
import adminCustomization from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/admin-customization.png";
import prototypeAdminCover from "../../assets/case-studies/public-ed-sites/prototype-admin-cover.png";

/**
 * The two prototypes embedded in the deck (slides 2057:11476 and 2057:11495),
 * as `figma.com/proto/…` share links shared "Anyone with the link can view".
 * Each embed renders only once its link is filled in.
 */
// The landing page's Figma prototype share link, to be added.
const LANDING_PROTOTYPE_URL = "";
// The admin's Figma prototype share link, to be added.
const ADMIN_PROTOTYPE_URL = "";
---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(publicEdSites)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Board management for non-profits"
    layout="stacked"
    image={boardableProduct}
    imageAlt="Boardable’s home page: board management software built for nonprofits"
    imageCaption="Boardable’s product: board management software for nonprofits."
  >
    <p>
      Boardable makes board management software for non-profits. Its users are
      the executive directors, administrators, and board members of non-profit
      organizations. The product covers video calls, structured meetings,
      minutes, and document management.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="A “hail mary”">
    <p>
      Board management is a crowded market with thin margins. Boardable had
      struggled in the past and cut product development. I was hired along with
      two product managers as part of an effort to reinvest in product, but the
      company’s big product bets were falling short.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then a partial competitor began discontinuing its public page product. We
      had exactly one month to launch a product of our own and seize that
      market.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="My role"
    image={replitPrototypeExample}
    imageAlt="A Replit workspace: an AI agent’s chat log beside a live preview of a board management prototype with upcoming meetings and discussions"
    imageCaption="An example of a Replit prototype, not the handoff from this project."
  >
    <p>
      This was a fast-execution project. Our chief product officer built a
      Replit prototype and set the MVP scope, and a product manager validated
      that feature set with customers. I was handed the work to execute: as the
      sole designer, I had a two-week window to design both the admin experience
      and the public-facing microsite.
    </p>
    <p>
      I designed the public site and the theming system, turned the prototype
      into a production-ready admin design, and handed it off to engineering.
      That included defining the customization options, and scoping the
      end-to-end experience so engineering could implement it in two to four
      weeks after handoff.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Public pages for education"
    layout="stacked"
    image={existingSites}
    imageAlt="An example community college website beside the competitor’s public page, which was being discontinued"
    imageCaption="A community college site beside the competitor’s public page, which was being discontinued."
  >
    <p>
      Schools and community colleges must publicly post their board meetings and
      policy books. I studied existing school and community college sites to
      identify familiar patterns and opportunities for improvement. That
      included the product being discontinued, as a benchmark, so customers
      switching from it would find a familiar structure.
    </p>
    <p>
      A few things stood out. Styling was dated and layouts were sub-optimal.
      Navigation was geared toward searching and scanning agendas. Introductory
      messages were important, but they sometimes crowded out higher-priority
      content like the agendas themselves.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Required features">
    <p>
      The public landing page needed a calendar of past and upcoming meetings
      and a searchable policy manual. The admin page needed ways to customize
      the look and feel, create and post a policy manual, and view activity and
      the list of meetings. Agendas needed an easy way to run the meeting and
      view attached documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Meeting creation would be handled through Boardable’s existing product.
      That left two surfaces to design: the public-facing, stand-alone site and
      the internally facing control panel.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Design system investment"
    layout="stacked"
    image={componentLibrary}
    imageAlt="The build-out in Figma, with screens laid out on the canvas"
    imageCaption="The first build-out in Figma, finished in four days from the component library."
  >
    <p>
      Alongside feature work, I maintained a component library for Boardable.
      That investment paid off here: it let me complete the initial build-out in
      four days.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Home page"
    image={homePage}
    imageAlt="A district home page with a header image, a welcome message, the next meeting, and featured meetings"
    imageCaption="The district home page: a header, a welcome, the next meeting, and featured meetings."
  >
    <p>
      The home page kept a simple information architecture. It highlighted the
      upcoming meeting, was built from modular sections, and ended in a footer
      with custom links.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also designed its empty states, for a site with no customizations, no
      featured meetings, or no policies, and its layout on phones.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: emptyNoCustomizations,
        alt: "Home page empty state with no customizations and no policies",
        caption: "Empty state: a site with no customizations and no policies.",
      },
      {
        image: emptyNoFeaturedMeetings,
        alt: "Home page empty state with no featured meetings",
        caption: "Empty state: no featured meetings.",
      },
      {
        image: emptyNoPolicies,
        alt: "Home page empty state with no policies",
        caption: "Empty state: no policies.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Theming"
    layout="stacked"
    image={theming}
    imageAlt="The same district site in several color themes"
    imageCaption="The same district site across the curated color themes."
  >
    <p>
      Theming went beyond the handoff. I added a customization system of curated
      themes built from header images, an extended color palette, and intro
      sections. I deliberately limited the options, based on my judgment of what
      most districts would need, so each site could look unique while the
      engineering scope stayed small.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Meetings list"
    layout="stacked"
    image={meetingsList}
    imageAlt="The meetings page, with the next meeting above a searchable, filterable list of all meetings"
    imageCaption="The meetings page: the next meeting, then a searchable list of the rest."
  >
    <p>
      The meetings list was searchable and filterable, and tags indicated
      whether each meeting was ready. Visitors could also view historical
      meetings.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Side-by-side agendas"
    layout="stacked"
    image={sideBySideAgenda}
    imageAlt="A meeting agenda in a full-screen modal, with the agenda outline beside its content and attached documents"
    imageCaption="A full-screen agenda, with the outline scrolling beside the content."
  >
    <p>
      Users indicated that being able to pull up an agenda side by side with its
      content was important. I designed the agenda to scroll along with the
      content, in a full-screen modal that reduces distraction during the
      meeting.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Policy book"
    image={policyBook}
    imageAlt="A district policy book with section jump links on the left"
    imageCaption="The policy book, with jump links between sections."
  >
    <p>
      The policy book was designed to be scannable and searchable. Jump links on
      the left move between sections, and search matches are highlighted inline,
      with jump links to each one.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Site-wide search"
    image={siteSearch}
    imageAlt="Search results mixing policies, agendas, and meetings, with filters in the left rail"
    imageCaption="Site-wide search across policies, agendas, and meetings."
  >
    <p>
      Site-wide search had to handle mixed content. Tabs divide the results by
      content type and use case, and filters in the left rail are more
      fine-grained. Each content type is visually distinct, and every result
      shows a preview of the matching text.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  {
    LANDING_PROTOTYPE_URL && (
      <DeckEmbed
        kind="prototype"
        title="Public Ed Sites landing page prototype"
        href={LANDING_PROTOTYPE_URL}
        cover={prototypeLandingCover}
        coverAlt="The landing page prototype: a district home page with the next meeting and featured meetings"
      />
    )
  }

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Admin control"
    layout="stacked"
    image={adminPolicies}
    imageAlt="The admin policy editor, with sections on the left and the selected item’s content on the right"
    imageCaption="The admin policy editor, laid out like the public policy book."
  >
    <p>
      I framed the admin dashboard as a module of the Boardable platform,
      leveraging navigation patterns and mental models I had established in an
      earlier project. Adding policies stayed close to the validated prototype.
      The editor mirrors the layout of the public policy book and offers several
      ways to reorganize the manual.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Customization"
    layout="stacked"
    image={adminCustomization}
    imageAlt="The customize site page, with header image, logo, and theme color settings"
    imageCaption="Site customization: a header image, a logo, and a theme color."
  >
    <p>
      Customization options stayed simple, with a basic preview. Admins set a
      header image, a logo, and a theme color, and can use a stock pattern
      instead of an image.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  {
    ADMIN_PROTOTYPE_URL && (
      <DeckEmbed
        kind="prototype"
        title="Public Ed Sites admin prototype"
        href={ADMIN_PROTOTYPE_URL}
        cover={prototypeAdminCover}
        coverAlt="The admin prototype: the public meetings list inside the Boardable platform"
      />
    )
  }

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>
      I delivered a high-fidelity experience in four working days, giving the
      team a real shot at the launch deadline. The product launched to customers
      and is still live today.
    </p>
    <p>
      Getting there meant trade-offs. I prioritized speed over nice-to-haves,
      limiting theming to a few curated options to keep the engineering scope
      small. I also relied on Boardable’s existing meeting creation and kept the
      admin close to the validated prototype, rather than redesigning either.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <Banner
    variant="section"
    title="Full deck available"
    body="The original presentation of this project, with more screens, including the responsive layouts."
    href="https://www.figma.com/deck/NNb1ZY5DqjMoAIKcsoWCuq/-case-study--Boardable-Public-Ed-Pages?node-id=0-1&t=FUXIgWlQA7ZlKM89-1"
    actionLabel="View the deck"
    image={deckCover}
    imageAlt="Title slide: Boardable Public Ed Sites, Morgan Keys, Summer 2025, beside a school district home page showing upcoming board meetings and policies"
  />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
